## Why This Is Asked

This question probes a beginner-friendly angle on data integrity under deploy pressure, focusing on per-tenant observability, controlled backpressure, and data replay safety.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant observability: lag, offsets, retry counts
- Ingress throttling and traffic-splitting
- Idempotent processing and dedup keys
- Rollback and data validation windows

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple deduplicated idempotent processor
const seen = new Set();
function handle(event){
  const id = event.tenant + ":" + event.id;
  if (seen.has(id)) return;
  seen.add(id);
  // process event
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the rollback plan in a staging env?
- What telemetry would you add to verify data integrity after rollback?

## Why This Is Asked
This probes practical fairness under backpressure, observability, and controlled rollout in production.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant fairness under burst
- Backpressure and throttling strategies
- Canary rollouts and rollback criteria
- Observability: per-tenant latency, backlog, saturation
- Budget-aware incident response

## Code Example
```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(ratePerSec, capacity){
    this.rate = ratePerSec;
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  tryConsume(n=1){
    const now = Date.now();
    const delta = (now - this.last)/1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + delta * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= n){ this.tokens -= n; return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate throttle fairness in a staging canary before rollout?
- What metrics would you collect for a postmortem and how would you communicate budget impact?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design automated degradation with controlled risk during external dependency pressure and multi-region operation.

## Key Concepts
- Circuit breakers and failover
- Per-region degradation strategies
- Idempotent queues and exactly-once delivery
- Canary and synthetic testing
- Rollback criteria and observability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for circuit breaker state machine
class CircuitBreaker {
  constructor(threshold, resetTime) {
    this.threshold = threshold;
    this.resetTime = resetTime;
    this.failures = 0;
    this.state = 'CLOSED';
  }
  onFailure() {
    this.failures++;
    if (this.failures > this.threshold) this.state = 'OPEN';
  }
  canRequest() { return this.state !== 'OPEN'; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for multi-tenant services with mixed SLIs?
- How would you validate degradation paths in canaries without impacting customers?
